   "source": [
    "# Exploring the Riemann Hypothesis\n",
    "\n",
    "This Jupyter notebook provides an interactive exploration of the **Riemann Hypothesis**, one of the most famous unsolved problems in mathematics. Proposed by Bernhard Riemann in 1859, the hypothesis states that all non-trivial zeros of the Riemann zeta function \\( \\zeta(s) \\) have a real part \\( \\text{Re}(s) = \\frac{1}{2} \\). This conjecture has profound implications for the distribution of prime numbers and connections to fields like quantum physics.\n",
    "\n",
    "## What You'll Learn\n",
    "\n",
    "In this notebook, we will:\n",
    "- Compute and visualize the non-trivial zeros of the Riemann zeta function on the critical line.\n",
    "- Explore the behavior of \\( \\zeta(s) \\) in the critical strip through visualizations.\n",
    "- Analyze the distribution of prime numbers and its connection to the Riemann Hypothesis.\n",
    "- Investigate the pair correlation of the zeros and its link to quantum chaos.\n",
    "- Provide references to further explore the topic within the **RiemannHypothesisExplorer** project.\n",
    "\n",
    "## About This Project\n",
    "\n",
    "This notebook is part of the **RiemannHypothesisExplorer** project, which includes:\n",
    "- A comprehensive PDF guide (`docs/riemann_hypothesis.pdf`).\n",
    "- Scripts for generating visualizations (`docs/scripts/`).\n",
    "- An interactive web calculator (`web_calculator/`).\n",
    "- And more! Check out the full project on [GitHub](https://github.com/[your-username]/RiemannHypothesisExplorer).\n",
    "\n",
    "Let's dive in!"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Import necessary libraries\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "from mpmath import zeta, zetazero\n",
    "import scipy.special as sp\n",
    "from utils.zeta_utils import compute_zeros\n",
    "from utils.plot_utils import set_plot_style\n",
    "\n",
    "# Set the plotting style for consistency\n",
    "set_plot_style()\n",
    "\n",
    "# Display a confirmation message\n",
    "print(\"Environment set up successfully! Ready to explore the Riemann Hypothesis.\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Computing Non-Trivial Zeros\n",
    "\n",
    "The Riemann zeta function \\( \\zeta(s) \\) is defined for complex numbers \\( s = \\sigma + it \\) as:\n",
    "\n",
    "\\[ \\zeta(s) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n^s} \\]\n",
    "\n",
    "for \\( \\text{Re}(s) > 1 \\), and by analytic continuation elsewhere. The **non-trivial zeros** of \\( \\zeta(s) \\) are the complex numbers \\( s \\) where \\( \\zeta(s) = 0 \\), excluding the trivial zeros at \\( s = -2, -4, -6, \\ldots \\). The Riemann Hypothesis conjectures that all non-trivial zeros lie on the **critical line** where \\( \\text{Re}(s) = \\frac{1}{2} \\).\n",
    "\n",
    "In this section, we will:\n",
    "- Compute the first 10 non-trivial zeros using the `mpmath` library.\n",
    "- Visualize them on the critical line \\( \\text{Re}(s) = \\frac{1}{2} \\).\n",
    "\n",
    "Let's compute the zeros!"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Compute the first 10 non-trivial zeros\n",
    "num_zeros = 10\n",
    "zeros = compute_zeros(num_zeros)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Print the zeros\n",
    "print(\"First 10 non-trivial zeros (imaginary parts):\")\n",
    "for i, z in enumerate(zeros, 1):\n",
    "    print(f\"Zero {i}: {z:.6f}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Plot the zeros on the critical line\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(6, 8))\n",
    "plt.scatter([0.5]*len(zeros), zeros, color=\"orange\", label=\"Non-Trivial Zeros\", s=100)\n",
    "plt.axvline(x=0.5, color=\"gray\", linestyle=\"--\", label=\"Critical Line (Re(s) = 1/2)\")\n",
    "plt.xlabel(\"Re(s)\")\n",
    "plt.ylabel(\"Im(s)\")\n",
    "plt.title(f\"First {num_zeros} Non-Trivial Zeros on the Critical Line\")\n",
    "plt.legend()\n",
    "plt.grid(True)\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Visualizing the Riemann Zeta Function\n",
    "\n",
    "To understand the behavior of \\( \\zeta(s) \\), we can visualize its magnitude in the **critical strip** (\\( 0 < \\sigma < 1 \\)) and its phase using domain coloring.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Magnitude in the Critical Strip\n",
    "\n",
    "We will plot \\( |\\zeta(\\sigma + it)| \\) for \\( \\sigma \\in [0, 1] \\) and \\( t \\in [0, 30] \\), which includes the critical line at \\( \\sigma = 0.5 \\). This will help us see where the zeros occur (where \\( |\\zeta(s)| = 0 \\)).\n",
    "\n",
    "### Domain Coloring\n",
    "\n",
    "Domain coloring visualizes a complex function by mapping its phase to a color and its magnitude to brightness. We will create a domain coloring plot of \\( \\zeta(s) \\) for \\( \\sigma \\in [-2, 2] \\) and \\( t \\in [0, 10] \\)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Define the range for the critical strip\n",
    "sigma_range = np.linspace(0, 1, 50)\n",
    "t_range = np.linspace(0, 30, 50)\n",
    "S, T = np.meshgrid(sigma_range, t_range)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Compute the magnitude of zeta(s)\n",
    "Z = np.zeros_like(S, dtype=complex)\n",
    "for i in range(S.shape[0]):\n",
    "    for j in range(S.shape[1]):\n",
    "        Z[i, j] = zeta(S[i, j] + 1j * T[i, j])\n",
    "\n",
    "# Plot the magnitude\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(10, 6))\n",
    "plt.contourf(S, T, np.abs(Z), levels=50, cmap=\"viridis\")\n",
    "plt.colorbar(label=\"|ζ(s)|\")\n",
    "plt.xlabel(\"σ\")\n",
    "plt.ylabel(\"t\")\n",
    "plt.title(\"Magnitude of ζ(s) in the Critical Strip\")\n",
    "plt.axvline(x=0.5, color=\"white\", linestyle=\"--\", label=\"Critical Line (σ = 1/2)\")\n",
    "plt.legend()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Define the range for domain coloring\n",
    "sigma_range = np.linspace(-2, 2, 100)\n",
    "t_range = np.linspace(0, 10, 100)\n",
    "S, T = np.meshgrid(sigma_range, t_range)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Compute zeta(s)\n",
    "Z = np.zeros_like(S, dtype=complex)\n",
    "for i in range(S.shape[0]):\n",
    "    for j in range(S.shape[1]):\n",
    "        Z[i, j] = zeta(S[i, j] + 1j * T[i, j])\n",
    "\n",
    "# Convert to HSV for domain coloring (phase to hue, magnitude to brightness)\n",
    "phase = np.angle(Z) / (2 * np.pi) + 0.5  # Map phase to [0, 1]\n",
    "magnitude = np.abs(Z)\n",
    "brightness = np.tanh(magnitude)  # Compress magnitude for visualization\n",
    "\n",
    "# Convert to RGB\n",
    "hsv = np.dstack((phase, np.ones_like(phase), brightness))\n",
    "from matplotlib.colors import hsv_to_rgb\n",
    "rgb = hsv_to_rgb(hsv)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Plot the domain coloring\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(10, 6))\n",
    "plt.imshow(rgb, extent=[-2, 2, 0, 10], aspect=\"auto\", origin=\"lower\")\n",
    "plt.xlabel(\"σ\")\n",
    "plt.ylabel(\"t\")\n",
    "plt.title(\"Domain Coloring of ζ(s)\")\n",
    "plt.axvline(x=0.5, color=\"white\", linestyle=\"--\", label=\"Critical Line (σ = 1/2)\")\n",
    "plt.legend()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Prime Number Distribution\n",
    "\n",
    "The Riemann Hypothesis is deeply connected to the distribution of prime numbers. The **prime counting function** \\( \\pi(x) \\) counts the number of primes less than or equal to \\( x \\). The Prime Number Theorem states that:\n",
    "\n",
    "\\[ \\pi(x) \\sim \\text{Li}(x) \\]\n",
    "\n",
    "where \\( \\text{Li}(x) = \\int_2^x \\frac{dt}{\\ln t} \\) is the logarithmic integral. The Riemann Hypothesis provides more precise bounds on the error term in this approximation.\n",
    "\n",
    "In this section, we will:\n",
    "- Compute \\( \\pi(x) \\) for \\( x \\) up to 1000.\n",
    "- Compare it with \\( \\text{Li}(x) \\).\n",
    "- Visualize the results to see the connection to the Riemann Hypothesis."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Define a function to compute π(x)\n",
    "def prime_counting(x):\n",
    "    \"\"\"Compute the number of primes less than or equal to x.\"\"\"\n",
    "    def is_prime(n):\n",
    "        if n < 2:\n",
    "            return False\n",
    "        for i in range(2, int(np.sqrt(n)) + 1):\n",
    "            if n % i == 0:\n",
    "                return False\n",
    "        return True\n",
    "    \n",
    "    return sum(1 for n in range(1, int(x) + 1) if is_prime(n))\n",
    "\n",
    "# Compute π(x) and Li(x)\n",
    "x_values = np.linspace(2, 1000, 999)\n",
    "pi_x = [prime_counting(x) for x in x_values]\n",
    "li_x = [sp.li(x) for x in x_values]\n",
    "\n",
    "# Plot the comparison\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(10, 6))\n",
    "plt.plot(x_values, pi_x, label=\"π(x) (Exact)\", color=\"blue\")\n",
    "plt.plot(x_values, li_x, label=\"Li(x) (Approximation)\", color=\"orange\", linestyle=\"--\")\n",
    "plt.xlabel(\"x\")\n",
    "plt.ylabel(\"Count\")\n",
    "plt.title(\"Prime Counting Function π(x) vs. Logarithmic Integral Li(x)\")\n",
    "plt.legend()\n",
    "plt.grid(True)\n",
    "plt.show()\n",
    "\n",
    "# Plot the error\n",
    "error = [pi - li for pi, li in zip(pi_x, li_x)]\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(10, 6))\n",
    "plt.plot(x_values, error, label=\"Error (π(x) - Li(x))\", color=\"red\")\n",
    "plt.xlabel(\"x\")\n",
    "plt.ylabel(\"Error\")\n",
    "plt.title(\"Error in Prime Counting Approximation\")\n",
    "plt.legend()\n",
    "plt.grid(True)\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Pair Correlation of Zeros\n",
    "\n",
    "The distribution of the non-trivial zeros exhibits fascinating statistical properties. The **pair correlation** of the zeros is conjectured to follow the same statistics as the eigenvalues of random Hermitian matrices in the Gaussian Unitary Ensemble (GUE), a concept from quantum chaos.\n",
    "\n",
    "In this section, we will:\n",
    "- Compute the normalized spacings between the first 1000 zeros.\n",
    "- Plot a histogram of the spacings and compare it with the GUE prediction."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Compute the first 1000 zeros\n",
    "num_zeros = 1000\n",
    "zeros = compute_zeros(num_zeros)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Compute the normalized spacings\n",
    "spacings = []\n",
    "for n in range(len(zeros)-1):\n",
    "    gamma_n = zeros[n]\n",
    "    gamma_np1 = zeros[n+1]\n",
    "    avg_spacing = (2 * np.pi) / np.log(gamma_n / (2 * np.pi))\n",
    "    s = (gamma_np1 - gamma_n) / avg_spacing\n",
    "    spacings.append(s)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Compute the histogram of spacings\n",
    "bins = np.linspace(0, 5, 50)\n",
    "hist, bin_edges = np.histogram(spacings, bins=bins, density=True)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Compute the GUE prediction\n",
    "s = np.linspace(0, 5, 100)\n",
    "gue = 1 - (np.sin(np.pi * s) / (np.pi * s))**2\n",
    "\n",
    "# Plot the results\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(10, 6))\n",
    "plt.plot(s, gue, label=\"GUE Prediction\", color=\"blue\", linewidth=2)\n",
    "plt.hist(spacings, bins=bins, density=True, alpha=0.5, label=\"Riemann Zeros\", color=\"orange\")\n",
    "plt.xlabel(\"Normalized Spacing (s)\")\n",
    "plt.ylabel(\"Probability Density\")\n",
    "plt.title(f\"Pair Correlation of the First {num_zeros} Riemann Zeros\")\n",
    "plt.legend()\n",
    "plt.grid(True)\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Connections to Quantum Physics\n",
    "\n",
    "The **Hilbert-Pólya conjecture** suggests that the non-trivial zeros of the Riemann zeta function might correspond to the eigenvalues of a quantum mechanical operator. This conjecture is motivated by the similarity between the spacing distribution of the zeros and the energy levels of quantum chaotic systems.\n",
    "\n",
    "In the previous section, we saw that the pair correlation of the zeros matches the GUE prediction. Let's further explore this by analyzing the spacing distribution of the zeros in more detail."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Compute the spacings (already done above)\n",
    "spacings = np.array(spacings)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Normalize the spacings to have mean 1\n",
    "spacings = spacings / np.mean(spacings)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Plot the spacing distribution\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(10, 6))\n",
    "plt.hist(spacings, bins=50, density=True, alpha=0.5, label=\"Spacing Distribution\", color=\"purple\")\n",
    "plt.xlabel(\"Normalized Spacing\")\n",
    "plt.ylabel(\"Probability Density\")\n",
    "plt.title(\"Spacing Distribution of Riemann Zeros\")\n",
    "plt.legend()\n",
    "plt.grid(True)\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
